<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="FB_EtherCATFrameDiag" Id="{c00b03c3-5a7a-47db-a857-8e703b702b2a}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    FB_EtherCATFrameDiag - working-counter (frame) health check for one EtherCAT frame.

    PURPOSE
        Condenses the EtherCAT master's per-frame WcState word into a single BOOL
        stating whether the datagrams the calling task depends on were processed
        by every expected slave. The caller links wFrmXWcState to the master's
        Inputs^FrmXWcState word (Frm1WcState for the first frame) and reads
        bFrameWcStateOK, which is TRUE only while none of the bits selected by
        wReqZeroMask is set.

    USAGE
        Called once per cycle from FB_EcatDiagWrapper, which forwards its own
        wFrmXWcState / wReqZeroMask and publishes the result as
        bFrameWcStateOK -> bAllFrameWcStatesOK. FB_EtherCATDiag consumes that flag
        as "all frames OK" and logs a Critical "Working Counter Frame Error"
        message when it drops, so this block is the cheapest early indication of
        a frame fault before the deep slave diagnostic is started.
        The block is stateless and combinational: the output follows the inputs in
        the same cycle, so it may be called from any task and as often as needed.
        The mask is per frame - keep the default 16#FFFF when every reported bit
        is a fault, or clear the bits that are unused or known benign on that
        master/frame.

    OBJECTIVES
        - Report a broken frame as one BOOL, so diagnostic code, the logger and
          EPICS do not each have to mask and compare the raw WcState word.
        - Keep the decision to ignore individual WcState bits in one explicit
          mask instead of scattering 16#.... literals through the call sites.
        - Stay side-effect free (no state, no timers, no logging) so the same
          block suits every frame and every task without configuration.

    HISTORY
        2020-03-15 / Alex Wallace
            Function block added ("Adding the frame diag because apparently it
            matters.") together with FB_EcatDiagWrapper.
        2026-11-27 / Scirix Agent
            Added this header comment and documented the declaration; comment
            only, no behaviour change (audit finding #96, convention
            conv_321640e095c0).

    NOTE
        The meaning of the individual WcState bits is defined by the EtherCAT
        master, not by this block: it only tests the masked bits against zero and
        never decodes them, so a set bit is reported as a frame fault without
        naming the slave whose working counter was missed.
*)
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_EtherCATFrameDiag
VAR_INPUT
    (*
        WcState word of the watched frame, taken from the EtherCAT master's
        Inputs^FrmXWcState (FrmXWcState for frame X). A set bit means the datagram
        of this frame was not processed as expected on the fieldbus; which bit
        means what is the master's definition, not this block's. No units - this
        is a status bitfield, not a counter.
    *)
    wFrmXWcState 		: WORD;				// frame WcState bitfield from the master, no units
    (*
        Mask of the wFrmXWcState bits that are checked:
        bit TRUE  = the bit must be FALSE for the frame to be reported OK,
        bit FALSE = the bit is ignored.
        Default 16#FFFF checks every bit; clear a bit only when that condition is
        unused or known benign on this frame.
    *)
    wReqZeroMask 		: WORD := 16#FFFF;	// checked-bit mask, default 16#FFFF (check every bit)
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    (*
        TRUE while every checked bit of wFrmXWcState is FALSE, i.e. the frame is
        healthy; FALSE as soon as one checked bit is set. Combinational, so it is
        valid in the same cycle the inputs are written.
    *)
    bFrameWcStateOK		: BOOL;				// all checked frame bits FALSE (frame OK)
END_VAR
VAR
END_VAR]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[(* mask out ignored error bits and compare result against 0 *)
bFrameWcStateOK := ((wFrmXWcState AND wReqZeroMask) = 0);]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>